<h1>Discover Northeast India: States & Indigenous Tribes</h1>

<section class="region-overview mb-2">
    <p>Northeast India, a breathtaking region comprising eight states, is a land of incredible diversity. From the snow-capped Himalayas in Sikkim and Arunachal Pradesh to the lush valleys of Assam and the rolling hills of Nagaland and Mizoram, the geography is as varied as its people. It's renowned for its unique cultural heritage, vibrant festivals, distinct cuisines, and being home to hundreds of indigenous communities.</p>
</section>

<hr class="mb-2">

<section class="states-overview">
    <h2>The Eight States of Northeast India</h2>
    <p>Often referred to as the "Seven Sisters" plus Sikkim, each state offers a unique glimpse into the region's character:</p>
    <ul class="states-list">
        <li><strong>Arunachal Pradesh:</strong> "Land of the Dawn-Lit Mountains," known for its stunning landscapes, numerous tribes (like Adi, Apatani, Nyishi, Monpa), and significant monasteries like Tawang.</li>
        <li><strong>Assam:</strong> Gateway to the Northeast, famous for its vast tea gardens, the mighty Brahmaputra river, Kaziranga National Park (home to the one-horned rhino), and the vibrant Bihu festival.</li>
        <li><strong>Manipur:</strong> The "Jewel of India," celebrated for its classical Manipuri dance, the unique Loktak Lake with floating islands (phumdis), the endangered Sangai deer, and rich cultural traditions.</li>
        <li><strong>Meghalaya:</strong> "Abode of the Clouds," home to the Khasi, Garo, and Jaintia tribes (many follow matrilineal traditions), famous for living root bridges, stunning waterfalls, and receiving some of the highest rainfall on Earth.</li>
        <li><strong>Mizoram:</strong> A land of rolling hills and serene landscapes, known for its high literacy rate, the close-knit Mizo society following the code of 'Tlawmngaihna', bamboo dance (Cheraw), and colorful festivals like Chapchar Kut.</li>
        <li><strong>Nagaland:</strong> The "Land of Festivals," inhabited by diverse Naga tribes (like Angami, Ao, Konyak, Sema), each with unique customs, known for its rich warrior heritage (historical), intricate weaves, and the famous Hornbill Festival.</li>
        <li><strong>Sikkim:</strong> Nestled in the Himalayas, offering spectacular views of Kanchenjunga, known for its Buddhist monasteries, serene environment, organic farming initiatives, and a blend of Lepcha, Bhutia, and Nepali cultures.</li>
        <li><strong>Tripura:</strong> A state blending plains and hills, known for its royal history (Neermahal palace), unique rock carvings (Unakoti), diverse tribal communities (like Tripuri, Reang/Bru, Jamatia), and rich bamboo crafts.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<hr class="mb-2">

<section class="tribes-showcase">
    <h2>Glimpses of Indigenous Tribes</h2>
    <p>The true heart of the Northeast lies in its diverse indigenous population. Below are just a few examples showcasing the region's incredible human tapestry:</p>

    <div class="tribe-grid">

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/E8E8E8/969696?text=Apatani+People" alt="Image representing the Apatani people">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Apatani (Arunachal Pradesh)</h3>
                <p>Residing primarily in the Ziro Valley, the Apatani are renowned for their highly efficient valley cultivation without farm animals, intricate weaving, and historically unique facial tattoos and nose plugs (now largely discontinued).</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/D0D0D0/969696?text=Bodo+Community" alt="Image representing the Bodo community">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Bodo (Assam)</h3>
                <p>The largest plains tribe of Assam, the Bodo people have a rich linguistic and cultural heritage. They are known for their vibrant Bwisagu festival, traditional Bathouism faith, and exquisite weaving, especially the Aronai scarf.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/E8E8E8/969696?text=Khasi+People" alt="Image representing the Khasi people">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Khasi (Meghalaya)</h3>
                <p>One of the major matrilineal communities in Meghalaya, the Khasi people inhabit the eastern hills. They speak a unique Austroasiatic language and are associated with the fascinating living root bridges found in the region.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/D0D0D0/969696?text=Garo+Community" alt="Image representing the Garo community">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Garo (Meghalaya)</h3>
                <p>Primarily inhabiting the Garo Hills, the Garo are another major matrilineal tribe of Meghalaya. They are known for their Wangala (100 Drums) harvest festival, traditional Nokpante bachelor dormitories, and distinct customs.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/E8E8E8/969696?text=Angami+Naga" alt="Image representing the Angami Naga people">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Angami Naga (Nagaland)</h3>
                <p>Known for their intricate terraced wet-rice cultivation near Kohima, the Angami are one of the prominent Naga tribes. Their Sekrenyi festival is a major annual event, and their traditional shawls feature distinct patterns.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/D0D0D0/969696?text=Konyak+Naga" alt="Image representing the Konyak Naga people">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Konyak Naga (Nagaland)</h3>
                <p>Residing mainly in Mon district, the Konyak are easily recognizable by their historical facial tattoos and traditional ear ornaments. They have a rich warrior past, a unique Angh chieftain system, and celebrate the Aoleang festival.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/E8E8E8/969696?text=Mizo+People" alt="Image representing the Mizo people">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Mizo (Mizoram)</h3>
                <p>A collective term for related tribes forming the majority in Mizoram, the Mizo are known for their strong community code (Tlawmngaihna), vibrant festivals like Chapchar Kut, and the iconic Cheraw (bamboo dance).</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="tribe-card">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/350x200/D0D0D0/969696?text=Tripuri+Community" alt="Image representing the Tripuri community">
            <div class="tribe-card-body">
                <h3>Tripuri (Tripura)</h3>
                <p>The original inhabitants and largest tribal community of Tripura, the Tripuri people (including Debbarma) have a rich history linked to the former princely state. Their Garia Puja and colourful traditional attire are notable.</p>
                <a href="#" class="card-link">Learn More (Soon)</a>
            </div>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

